he region of the upper and middle Niger in West Africa, now the Republic of Mali.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mali-conquest-of-koumbi-saleh"><span class="enc-name">Mali conquest of Koumbi Saleh</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 1240</span><span class="enc-snip">The Mali conquest of Koumbi Saleh in 1240 was the capture of the former capital of the Ghana Empire by the forces of Sundiata Keita.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mali-empire.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mali Empire</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">The Mali Empire was a West African empire founded by Sundiata Keita around 1235, which flourished until the fifteenth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mali-federation"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mali-federation.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mali Federation</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">The Mali Federation was a union of Senegal and the Sudanese Republic, the former French Sudan, formed in 1959 within the French Community.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/malietoa-laupepa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/malietoa-laupepa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Malietoa Laupepa</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Malietoa Laupepa was a Samoan paramount chief recognised as King of Samoa for much of the late 19th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/malietoa-tanumafili-i"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/malietoa-tanumafili-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Malietoa Tanumafili I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Malietoa Tanumafili I was a Samoan paramount chief, son of Malietoa Laupepa.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-malik-al-rahim"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/al-malik-al-rahim.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">al-Malik al-Rahim</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Al-Malik al-Rahim was the last Buyid ruler of Iraq, succeeding his father Abu Kalijar in 1048.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/malik-ambar"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/malik-ambar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Malik Ambar</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Malik Ambar was an Ethiopian-born soldier, brought to India as a slave, who became regent of the Ahmadnagar Sultanate.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/malik-ashraf"><span class="enc-name">Malik Ashraf</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Malik Ashraf was a Chupanid ruler who controlled Tabriz and much of north-western Iran after the murder of his brother Hasan Kuchak in 1343.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/malik-kafur"><span class="enc-name">Malik Kafur</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Malik Kafur was a general of Alauddin Khalji, sultan of Delhi, in the early fourteenth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/malik-muhammad-jayasi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/malik-muhammad-jayasi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Malik Muhammad Jayasi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Malik Muhammad Jayasi was a Sufi poet from Jais in Awadh who wrote in the Awadhi language.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a hre