erson</span><span class="enc-snip">Malik Shah I was sultan of the Seljuk Empire from 1072 until his death in 1092, when the empire reached its greatest extent.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/maliki-school.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Maliki school</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">The Maliki school is one of the four Sunni schools of Islamic law, named after the jurist Malik ibn Anas of Medina.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/maliseet"><span class="enc-name">Maliseet</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Wolastoqiyik, also known as the Maliseet, are an Algonquian-speaking First Nation of the Saint John River valley in New Brunswick and neighbouring Quebec and Maine.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/malka-river"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/malka-river.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Malka (river)</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Malka is a river of the North Caucasus in Russia, about 216 km long, rising on the northern slopes of Mount Elbrus and flowing east and north-east through…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/malkikarib-yuhamin"><span class="enc-name">Malkikarib Yuha'min</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Malkikarib Yuha'min was a king of Himyar in south Arabia in the late 4th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mallam-dendo"><span class="enc-name">Mallam Dendo</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mallam Dendo was a Fulani scholar and one of the flag-bearers of Usman dan Fodio's jihad in the early nineteenth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href=